WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 324: George I: The German Elector, the South Sea Bubble, and Sir Robert Walpole

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.880
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the foreign prince who transformed the constitutional architecture of Great Britain.

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.200
[Rebecca] A sovereign who spoke little English, yet under whose reign the modern office of Prime Minister was born.

5
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:41.360
[George] Born in May 1660 at Osnabruck in northern Germany, Georg Ludwig was the eldest son of Ernst August, Duke of Brunswick Lunberg, and Sophia of the Palatinate, granddaughter of King James I.

6
00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:54.400
[George] Reared in the militarized German princely states during the height of the Ottoman and French wars, George was a seasoned, courageous soldier who fought alongside the Holy Roman Emperor at the Siege of Vienna in 1683.

7
00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:02.080
[George] In 1682, George married his cousin, Sophia Dorothea of Celle, for territorial consolidation.

8
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:12.080
[George] The marriage was an unmitigated personal disaster, when Sophia Dorothea engaged in a scandalous affair with the dashing Swedish Count Philip Christoph von Kernigsmark.

9
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:23.920
[George] Koenigsmark was mysteriously assassinated in the palace at Hanover, and George divorced Sophia Dorothea, imprisoning her in the fortress of Alden Castle for 32 years until her death.

10
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:37.680
[George] In 1698, George became Elector of Hanover, governing his German Principality with exemplary fiscal prudence from his Baroque palace of Herrenhausen, taking immense pride in its grand fountains and hedge mazes.

11
00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.760
[George] The tragic fate of Sophia Dorothea of Cell remained the darkest scandal of George's private life.

12
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:52.080
[George] Koenigsmark, a handsome Swedish nobleman, had wooed the lonely, neglected electress, plotting to help her escape from Hanover.

13
00:01:52.080 --> 00:02:05.800
[George] On the night of July 1, 1694, Koenigsmark was intercepted leaving the Lanischlos Palace by four of the Elector's guards, run through with swords, and his body dumped into the river or buried beneath palace floorboards.

14
00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:18.840
[George] For thirty-two years, Sophia Dorothea was locked in the moted castle of Alden, forbidden from seeing her children or remarrying, allowed to ride her carriage only within a two-mile perimeter under armed guard.

15
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:26.920
[George] This cold, unforgiving severity earned George the enduring hatred of his son George Augustus and the horror of British society.

16
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:35.640
[George] When Queen Anne died in August 1714, the British Parliament enacted the most radical dynastic engineering in world history.

17
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.960
[George] Under the 1701 Act of Settlement, over fifty closer Catholic relatives, including James I.

18
00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:51.320
[George] I's exiled son, the old pretender, were disqualified on religious grounds, leaving 54-year-old George as the closest Protestant heir.

19
00:02:51.320 --> 00:03:06.360
[George] On September 18, 1714, George landed at Greenwich through thick autumn fog aboard the royal yacht Peregrine, accompanied by his son George Augustus and a retinue of German courtiers and Turkish valets, Mahomet and Mustafa.

20
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:18.920
[George] Crowned at Westminster Abbey in October in a ceremony conducted largely in Latin, because the king spoke minimal English and his Whig ministers spoke no German, George was greeted with skepticism by Londoners.

21
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:31.880
[George] Satirists mocked his two German mistresses, the tall, gaunt Melusign von der Schulenberg, whom Londoners nicknamed the Maypole, and the corpulent Sophia von Kielmansegg, nicknamed the Elephant.

22
00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:38.600
[George] Yet George was an experienced, pragmatic European ruler who understood his limited constitutional role.

23
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:48.240
[George] George I's coronation at Westminster Abbey was a linguistic comedy of errors, because the German king knew very little English and the Whig ministers knew no German.

24
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:56.400
[George] Proceedings were conducted in broken, kitchen Latin, with both sides frequently misunderstanding ceremonial instructions.

25
00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:03.840
[George] Outside the abbey, Jacobite riots erupted in London, Bristol, and Norwich, with crowds shouting No Hanover.

26
00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.680
[George] High Church and Ormond.

27
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.160
[George] Yet George was far from a dullard.

28
00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:22.000
[George] He was an accomplished mathematician, a patron of George Fryderick Handel, who composed the magnificent water music for the King's Royal Barge Progress on the Thames in 1717, and a monarch of calm, unflappable dignity.

29
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:30.160
[George] The Hanovarian succession was immediately challenged in September 1715 by the Jacobite Rising, known as the 15.

30
00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:41.920
[George] John Erskine, Earl of Marr, raised the Stuart standard in the Scottish Highlands, rallying 10,000 Highland clansmen to restore James Francis Edward Stuart, the old pretender.

31
00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:46.560
[George] Concurrently, Northern English Catholic gentry rose in Northumberland.

32
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.280
[George] But the rebellion was fatally mismanaged.

33
00:04:49.280 --> 00:05:02.160
[George] The English Jacobites were surrounded and surrendered at the Battle of Preston in Lancashire, while Mars Highland army fought a bloody, indecisive stalemate against the Duke of Argyll's Hanovarian forces at Sheriff Muir in November.

34
00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:10.320
[George] When the old pretender finally landed at Peterhead in December, he was melancholic, uninspiring, and lacked money and arms.

35
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:20.240
[George] By February 1716, as Hanoverian reinforcements advanced through the winter snows, James fled back to France aboard a small ship from Montrose.

36
00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:28.000
[George] George I acted with calculated firmness, while key Jacobite lords like the Earl of Derwentwaiter were beheaded on Tower Hill.

37
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:33.560
[George] The king pardoned hundreds of common prisoners, securing his throne against domestic rebellion.

38
00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:44.120
[George] The Jacobite Rising of 1715 was doomed by the military incompetence of the Earl of Marr, affectionately satirized as Bobbing John for his shifting loyalties.

39
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:53.880
[George] Despite having an army that vastly outnumbered government forces in Scotland, Maher hesitated at Sheriff Muir, allowing the Duke of Argyll to hold the field.

40
00:05:53.880 --> 00:06:07.720
[George] When James Francis Edward Stewart arrived, he suffered from severe depression and toothache, failing to inspire his Highland followers, remarking mournfully that he had come to share their funeral rather than celebrate a triumph.

41
00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:21.480
[George] George I's firm suppression of the rebellion, followed by the disarming act and the construction of military roads across the highlands by General George Wade, firmly consolidated the Hanoverian dynasty upon the British throne.

42
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:30.120
[George] In 1720, Great Britain was rocked by the first colossal stock market crash in financial history, the South Sea Bubble.

43
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:44.520
[George] The South Sea Company, founded to trade slaves and goods with Spanish America, proposed an audacious scheme to assume £31 million of Britain's national debt in exchange for exclusive trading monopolies and stock conversions.

44
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.160
[George] Speculative hysteria gripped London.

45
00:06:47.160 --> 00:07:00.280
[George] Aristocrats, politicians, clergymen, and ordinary citizens crowded into Exchange Alley to buy shares, which skyrocketed from £128 in January to over £1,000 in August.

46
00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.840
[George] The royal court was deeply implicated.

47
00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:08.280
[George] George I's German mistresses and ministers accepted corrupt bribes of unissued stock.

48
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:17.040
[George] When the unsustainable speculative bubble burst in September 1720, share prices collapsed to 135 pounds in weeks.

49
00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:26.240
[George] Thousands of families were ruined, suicides swept London, and public fury threatened to overthrow both the government and the Hanoverian monarchy.

50
00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:32.320
[George] The South Sea bubble exposed the raw venality and speculative mania of early Georgian capitalism.

51
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:47.920
[George] Promoters launched absurd joint stock companies in Exchange Alley, including schemes to extract silver from lead, build floating mansions, and most famously, a company for carrying on an undertaking of great advantage, but nobody to know what it is.

52
00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.720
[George] Which collected thousands of pounds in deposits before the promoter vanished overnight.

53
00:07:52.720 --> 00:08:03.120
[George] When the bubble burst, Sir Isaac Newton, who lost 20,000 pounds in South Sea stock, famously remarked, I can calculate the motions of heavenly bodies.

54
00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.200
[George] But not the madness of people.

55
00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:11.280
[George] George I himself had made profits on early share sales, narrowly avoiding public disgrace.

56
00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:20.320
[George] In the midst of the financial catastrophe, King George turned to Sir Robert Walpole, a genial, shrewd Norfolk squire and financial genius.

57
00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:28.400
[George] Appointed first Lord of the Treasury and Chancellor of the Exchequer in April 1721, Walpole implemented the screening operation.

58
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:39.440
[George] He restructured the ruined company's debts, dividing the loss between the Bank of England and the East India Company, while shielding the royal family and ministers from public prosecution.

59
00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.000
[George] Walpole restored national credit and stabilized the British economy.

60
00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:55.920
[George] Because George I preferred residing in Hanover, spending nearly a fifth of his reign abroad, and found British parliamentary debates tedious, the king stopped attending regular cabinet meetings.

61
00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:06.280
[George] In the king's absence, Walpole chaired the meetings, coordinated government policy, and commanded majorities in the House of Commons through patronage and political management.

62
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:15.000
[George] Walpole thus became Britain's first prime minister, permanently shifting executive governing power from the royal bedchamber to Downing Street.

63
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:20.840
[George] Sir Robert Walpole's political genius lay in his earthy, cynical understanding of human nature.

64
00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:25.960
[George] He famously observed of parliamentarians, all those men have their price.

65
00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:37.160
[George] Operating from 10 Downing Street, which George I gifted to Walpole in 1735 as an official residence for the first Lord of the Treasury, Walpole established cabinet solidarity.

66
00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:42.040
[George] Insisting that all ministers publicly support government policy or resign.

67
00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:51.400
[George] Walpole avoided costly foreign wars, kept land taxes low for country squires, and coined the famous maxim of British political stability.

68
00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:54.920
[George] Quieta non-mover, let sleeping dogs lie.

69
00:09:54.920 --> 00:10:03.720
[George] Through his twenty-one years as Britain's first prime minister, Walpole forged the constitutional machinery of modern British parliamentary democracy.

70
00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:10.520
[George] A persistent curse of the Hanoverian dynasty was the toxic, venomous hatred between fathers and their sons.

71
00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:21.720
[George] George I despised his eldest son, George Augustus, Prince of Wales, who was popular, fluent in English, and fiercely protective of his imprisoned mother Sophia Dorothea.

72
00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:38.600
[George] The feud exploded into public scandal in November 1717 at the christening of the prince's newborn son, when the prince quarreled violently with the Duke of Newcastle, whom the king had appointed godfather, King George placed his son under house arrest and banished him from St.

73
00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.280
[George] James's Palace.

74
00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:50.000
[George] The Prince of Wales established a rival court at Leicester House, which became the headquarters for opposition Whig and Tory politicians plotting against the King's government.

75
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.840
[George] This established the enduring Hanovarian political pattern.

76
00:10:53.840 --> 00:11:01.040
[George] The heir to the throne always sided with the parliamentary opposition against his father, dividing the political establishment.

77
00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:09.280
[George] The feud between George I and the Prince of Wales at Leicester House was an embarrassing public spectacle that paralyzed London society.

78
00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:15.440
[George] The king issued a royal decree forbidding anyone who attended the prince's court from being received at St.

79
00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:20.400
[George] James's Palace, forcing aristocrats to choose between father and son.

80
00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:33.280
[George] The Princess of Wales, Caroline of Ansbach, used her dazzling salon at Leicester House to patronise poets like Alexander Pope and John Gay, transforming the opposition court into the cultural centre of London.

81
00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:44.320
[George] Ridiculing the King's stiff German mistresses and parsimonious court habits, deepening the family rift until Walpole brokered an uneasy public reconciliation in 1720.

82
00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:53.520
[George] In June 1727, 67-year-old King George I set out from London on his sixth journey home to his beloved electorate of Hanover.

83
00:11:53.520 --> 00:12:06.240
[George] Travelling across the Dutch countryside, the King was in high spirits, but on the road between Delden and Nordhorn on June 9, he suffered a severe stroke in his carriage, his face becoming paralyzed.

84
00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:17.520
[George] When his courtiers urged stopping at the nearest town for medical treatment, the king stubbornly muttered in German, Osnabruck, Osnabruck, desperate to reach his birthplace.

85
00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:26.800
[George] Arriving at the Prince Bishop's Palace in Osnabruck, George I was carried into his bedchamber, where he died on June 11, 1727.

86
00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:34.680
[George] He was buried in the chapel of Lane Castle in Hanover, the only British monarch since 1066 to be buried outside the British Isles.

87
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:48.520
[George] George I was never deeply loved by his British subjects, yet his pragmatism, emotional detachment, and constitutional restraint had allowed parliamentary cabinet government to take root, securing the Protestant succession.

88
00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:57.560
[George] When George I suffered his fatal stroke in his carriage near Osnabruck, his passing marked the quiet completion of Britain's constitutional revolution.

89
00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:09.160
[George] By leaving the day-to-day administration of the realm to his cabinet and parliament, George I had unwittingly facilitated the transition from royal personal rule to modern constitutional monarchy.

90
00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:23.320
[George] The British Crown had become an institution that reigned rather than ruled, preserving national stability while allowing the dynamic commercial, maritime, and intellectual energies of the British people to build the richest empire on earth.

91
00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.880
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

92
00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:42.840
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of George I, the German Elector, the South Sea Bubble, and Sir Robert Walpole, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

93
00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:52.680
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

